From what time I've spent with threads in Java, I've found these two ways to write threads:

The primary difference between implementing `Runnable` and extending `Thread` lies in the way you can create and start instances of these classes.

When you implement `Runnable`, you can create a new instance of your class like this:

```java
MyRunnable myRunnable = new MyRunnable();
```

And start it like this:

```java
myRunnable.run();
```

On the other hand, when you extend `Thread`, you create a new class that already has the `run()` method defined, so you don't need to implement anything:

```java
public class MyThread extends Thread {
    public MyThread() {
        super("MyThread");
    }
    public void run() {
        //Code
    }
}
```

This is more concise and easier to read, especially when you have a lot of code to put into the `run()` method. However, extending `Thread` has some limitations - you can't override any of the methods from the `Thread` class, and you can't use the `start()` method.

